{#
  T-2.06 — the text, phone and textarea field.

  Usage:
    {% include 'components.ui.input' with {
        id: 'phone', label: trans('…'), type: 'tel', required: true,
        autocomplete: 'tel', hint: trans('…'), error: errors.phone
    } only %}

  Parameters:
    id            required — ties the label, the field, the hint and the error together
    label         required — visible, and programmatically associated. Never a placeholder
    type          text (default) · tel · email · number · password · search
    tag           'input' (default) or 'textarea'
    name          defaults to `id`
    value         prefill
    placeholder   optional — an EXAMPLE, never a substitute for the label
    hint          optional — help text, linked by `aria-describedby`
    error         optional — a message; also flips `aria-invalid`
    required      true → `required` + a marked label
    disabled      true → the native attribute, and the dimmed treatment
    autocomplete  the token; see WHY AUTOCOMPLETE IS A PARAMETER below
    inputmode     defaults sensibly from `type`
    rows          textarea only, default 4

  `disabled` WAS ADDED BY T-2.16's AUDIT, and it uses the NATIVE attribute where
  the button partial deliberately uses `aria-disabled`. The two are not
  inconsistent, they are two different requirements: a disabled button must only
  refuse activation, so it stays in the tab order and stays announceable; a
  disabled **field** must also not submit its value, and that is a semantic only
  the attribute carries. `choice.twig` made the same call in T-2.08.

  A PLACEHOLDER IS NOT A LABEL, and this partial cannot be used as though it
  were: `label` is required and always rendered. A placeholder disappears the
  moment someone types, taking the field's name with it — which is a problem for
  everyone and a barrier for anyone using magnification or a screen reader.

  THE ERROR IS LINKED, NOT JUST RED. `aria-describedby` points at it, `aria-invalid`
  marks the field, and the message carries `role="alert"` so it is announced when
  it appears. Colour alone would fail doc 13; so would a red border alone.

  WHY `autocomplete` IS A PARAMETER AND NOT A GUESS. WCAG 1.3.5 asks that fields
  collecting the user's own data identify their purpose, and only the caller
  knows whether a `tel` field is the customer's mobile or the shop's. Passing it
  is one word; inferring it would be wrong on the field that matters.

  RTL AND LATIN DIGITS. A phone number is a left-to-right run inside a
  right-to-left page. `dir="ltr"` on the INPUT — an inline box of its own, never
  its container — is T-1.04's rule applied: isolation goes on the run, and a
  `dir` on the wrapper would re-resolve the label's alignment to the wrong edge.
#}
{% set field_tag = tag|default('input') %}
{% set field_type = type|default('text') %}
{% set field_name = name|default(id) %}
{% set field_required = required|default(false) %}
{% set field_has_error = error is defined and error is not empty %}
{% set field_has_hint = hint is defined and hint is not empty %}
{% set field_disabled = disabled|default(false) %}
{% set field_ltr = field_type in ['tel', 'number', 'email', 'password'] %}
{% set field_described = (field_has_hint ? id ~ '-hint ' : '') ~ (field_has_error ? id ~ '-error' : '') %}

<div class="field {{ field_has_error ? 'field--invalid' : '' }} {{ field_disabled ? 'field--disabled' : '' }} {{ class|default('') }}">
    <label class="field__label" for="{{ id }}">
        {{ label }}
        {#
          Required is carried by the `required` attribute, which is what a screen
          reader announces. The asterisk is for everyone else, and it is given a
          word of its own rather than left as a symbol whose meaning is folklore.
        #}
        {% if field_required %}
            <span class="field__required" aria-hidden="true">*</span>
            <span class="sr-only">{{ trans('theme.form.required') }}</span>
        {% endif %}
    </label>

    {% if field_tag == 'textarea' %}
        <textarea class="field__input field__input--area"
                  id="{{ id }}"
                  name="{{ field_name }}"
                  rows="{{ rows|default(4) }}"
                  {% if placeholder is defined and placeholder is not empty %}placeholder="{{ placeholder }}"{% endif %}
                  {% if autocomplete is defined and autocomplete is not empty %}autocomplete="{{ autocomplete }}"{% endif %}
                  {% if field_required %}required aria-required="true"{% endif %}
                  {% if field_disabled %}disabled{% endif %}
                  {% if field_has_error %}aria-invalid="true"{% endif %}
                  {% if field_described %}aria-describedby="{{ field_described|trim }}"{% endif %}>{{ value|default('') }}</textarea>
    {% else %}
        <input class="field__input {{ field_ltr ? 'field__input--ltr' : '' }}"
               id="{{ id }}"
               name="{{ field_name }}"
               type="{{ field_type }}"
               value="{{ value|default('') }}"
               {% if field_ltr %}dir="ltr"{% endif %}
               {% if inputmode is defined and inputmode is not empty %}inputmode="{{ inputmode }}"
               {% elseif field_type == 'tel' %}inputmode="tel"
               {% elseif field_type == 'number' %}inputmode="numeric"
               {% elseif field_type == 'email' %}inputmode="email"{% endif %}
               {% if placeholder is defined and placeholder is not empty %}placeholder="{{ placeholder }}"{% endif %}
               {% if autocomplete is defined and autocomplete is not empty %}autocomplete="{{ autocomplete }}"{% endif %}
               {% if field_required %}required aria-required="true"{% endif %}
               {% if field_disabled %}disabled{% endif %}
               {% if field_has_error %}aria-invalid="true"{% endif %}
               {% if field_described %}aria-describedby="{{ field_described|trim }}"{% endif %}>
    {% endif %}

    {% if field_has_hint %}
        <p class="field__hint" id="{{ id }}-hint">{{ hint }}</p>
    {% endif %}

    {#
      `role="alert"` and not `aria-live="polite"`: a validation message appears in
      response to something the user just did, and it is the reason their action
      did not complete. It should interrupt.
    #}
    {% if field_has_error %}
        <p class="field__error" id="{{ id }}-error" role="alert">
            {% include 'components.ui.icon' with { name: 'cancel-circle' } only %}
            {{ error }}
        </p>
    {% endif %}
</div>
